1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,434
A merry-go-round: the outer horse
races, the inner one strolls.

2
00:00:04,434 --> 00:00:07,630
A fan: the tip blurs,
the centre barely moves.

3
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:10,217
Each one turns as a single piece.

4
00:00:10,217 --> 00:00:12,309
So what do all its parts share?

5
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:15,399
Aarav spins his upside-down bicycle wheel.

6
00:00:15,399 --> 00:00:20,054
The valve near the rim and the reflector
near the hub go round together.

7
00:00:20,404 --> 00:00:22,599
The valve covers far more distance.

8
00:00:22,599 --> 00:00:24,608
So which one is turning faster?

9
00:00:25,108 --> 00:00:28,895
To answer that, we measure
turning by angle, not by distance.

10
00:00:29,545 --> 00:00:31,650
Let's start with angular position.

11
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,577
Draw a line from the centre to the point.

12
00:00:34,577 --> 00:00:38,678
Its angle from a fixed reference
line is the angular position, theta.

13
00:00:39,078 --> 00:00:41,455
As the wheel turns, theta changes.

14
00:00:41,455 --> 00:00:44,984
The change, delta theta, is
the angular displacement.

15
00:00:44,984 --> 00:00:47,095
Anticlockwise counts as positive.

16
00:00:47,495 --> 00:00:49,387
We measure it in radians.

17
00:00:49,387 --> 00:00:54,328
One radian is the angle where the
arc is exactly as long as the radius.

18
00:00:54,728 --> 00:00:57,018
One full turn is two pi radians.

19
00:00:57,018 --> 00:00:59,712
That is three hundred and sixty degrees.

20
00:01:00,312 --> 00:01:03,403
Let's spin Aarav's wheel
in our motion notebook.

21
00:01:03,403 --> 00:01:06,239
It makes one full turn every two seconds.

22
00:01:06,589 --> 00:01:09,606
The reflector sits ten
centimetres from the centre.

23
00:01:09,606 --> 00:01:11,915
The valve sits thirty centimetres out.

24
00:01:12,315 --> 00:01:13,458
Start the clock.

25
00:01:13,458 --> 00:01:17,231
After half a second, both have
turned through the same angle.

26
00:01:17,231 --> 00:01:19,527
A quarter turn, pi by two radians.

27
00:01:19,927 --> 00:01:23,837
But the valve's arc is three times
longer than the reflector's arc.

28
00:01:24,337 --> 00:01:25,524
Keep it spinning.

29
00:01:25,524 --> 00:01:29,957
On the graph, the angle grows along
one straight line, for both points.

30
00:01:30,357 --> 00:01:31,706
Let's slow it down.

31
00:01:32,056 --> 00:01:34,854
The valve's velocity arrow
is three times longer.

32
00:01:34,854 --> 00:01:38,064
But both arrows turn together,
through the same angle.

33
00:01:38,464 --> 00:01:40,705
So the angle is what they share.

34
00:01:40,705 --> 00:01:44,052
How fast the angle grows
is called angular velocity.

35
00:01:44,702 --> 00:01:47,269
Let's write it down, one step at a time.

36
00:01:47,619 --> 00:01:48,553
Step one.

37
00:01:48,553 --> 00:01:52,973
Angular velocity, omega, is
angular displacement over time.

38
00:01:52,973 --> 00:01:57,599
Omega equals delta theta over
delta t, in radians per second.

39
00:01:58,349 --> 00:01:59,265
Step two.

40
00:01:59,265 --> 00:02:04,817
For a point at radius r, the arc it
covers is s equals r theta, with theta

41
00:02:04,817 --> 00:02:05,845
in radians.

42
00:02:06,595 --> 00:02:07,601
Step three.

43
00:02:07,601 --> 00:02:09,909
Divide both sides by the time.

44
00:02:09,909 --> 00:02:13,933
s over t is the speed v,
and theta over t is omega.

45
00:02:14,683 --> 00:02:16,311
So v equals r omega.

46
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:19,872
The same omega, a bigger
radius, a bigger speed.

47
00:02:20,622 --> 00:02:21,597
Step four.

48
00:02:21,597 --> 00:02:25,308
One full turn is two pi
radians in one period T.

49
00:02:25,308 --> 00:02:29,310
So omega equals two pi
over T, which is two pi nu.

50
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:34,619
For Aarav's wheel, two pi over two
seconds is pi radians per second.

51
00:02:35,269 --> 00:02:36,404
Remember this.

52
00:02:36,404 --> 00:02:39,957
Every point on a turning
wheel has the same omega.

53
00:02:39,957 --> 00:02:41,654
And v equals r omega.

54
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:43,767
Side by side now.

55
00:02:44,117 --> 00:02:45,321
The reflector.

56
00:02:45,321 --> 00:02:50,473
Radius ten centimetres, omega pi
radians per second, so v is zero point

57
00:02:50,473 --> 00:02:52,667
three one metres per second.

58
00:02:53,067 --> 00:02:53,973
The valve.

59
00:02:53,973 --> 00:02:59,004
Radius thirty centimetres, the same
omega, so v is zero point nine four.

60
00:02:59,004 --> 00:03:02,265
Three times the radius,
three times the speed.

61
00:03:02,865 --> 00:03:03,871
Now you try.

62
00:03:03,871 --> 00:03:08,103
On a clock, the second hand's
tip, or a point halfway along it.

63
00:03:08,103 --> 00:03:10,557
Which has the bigger angular velocity?

64
00:03:10,557 --> 00:03:11,808
Pause and decide.

65
00:03:15,008 --> 00:03:15,908
The same!

66
00:03:15,865 --> 00:03:19,891
Both sweep three hundred and
sixty degrees in sixty seconds.

67
00:03:19,891 --> 00:03:21,711
Only their speeds differ.

68
00:03:22,361 --> 00:03:23,261
One more.

69
00:03:23,168 --> 00:03:25,599
Aarav spins the wheel twice as fast.

70
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:27,823
What happens to the valve's speed?

71
00:03:27,823 --> 00:03:29,034
Pause and think.

72
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:33,252
It doubles!

73
00:03:33,252 --> 00:03:34,736
v equals r omega.

74
00:03:34,736 --> 00:03:39,287
The radius stays the same, so
double omega gives double speed.

75
00:03:39,937 --> 00:03:41,673
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:42,023 --> 00:03:45,209
A ceiling fan makes three
hundred turns a minute.

77
00:03:45,209 --> 00:03:48,100
Its blade tip is zero
point six metres out.

78
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:50,175
Find omega and the tip's speed.

79
00:03:50,675 --> 00:03:54,272
Three hundred turns a minute
is five turns each second.

80
00:03:54,272 --> 00:03:55,820
So nu is five hertz.

81
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:58,422
Omega equals two pi nu.

82
00:03:58,422 --> 00:04:03,019
That is ten pi, about thirty one
point four radians per second.

83
00:04:03,769 --> 00:04:05,299
v equals r omega.

84
00:04:05,299 --> 00:04:09,548
That is zero point six times
ten pi, which is six pi.

85
00:04:10,298 --> 00:04:13,727
About eighteen point eight
metres per second at the tip.

86
00:04:13,727 --> 00:04:16,537
Every point on the blade
has the same omega.

87
00:04:17,187 --> 00:04:18,652
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:04:19,002 --> 00:04:22,857
Many students put degrees
straight into v equals r omega.

89
00:04:23,207 --> 00:04:26,310
But s equals r theta
works only in radians.

90
00:04:26,310 --> 00:04:30,110
One eighty degrees per second
is pi radians per second.

91
00:04:30,110 --> 00:04:34,901
So the valve moves at zero point nine
four metres per second, not fifty

92
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:35,801
four.

93
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:37,652
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:37,652 --> 00:04:42,405
As quick numericals: clock hands,
fan blades, and r p m to radians per

95
00:04:42,405 --> 00:04:43,305
second.

96
00:04:43,674 --> 00:04:44,867
Exam level now.

97
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:47,879
A clock's minute hand
is six centimetres long.

98
00:04:47,879 --> 00:04:51,467
Find its angular velocity,
and the speed of its tip.

99
00:04:51,467 --> 00:04:52,710
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:58,110 --> 00:04:59,485
The answer is B.

101
00:04:59,985 --> 00:05:04,720
The minute hand turns two pi radians
in one hour, three thousand six hundred

102
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:05,620
seconds.

103
00:05:05,922 --> 00:05:11,110
So omega is two pi over thirty six
hundred, which is pi over eighteen hundred

104
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:12,571
radians per second.

105
00:05:13,071 --> 00:05:15,123
The tip's speed is r omega.

106
00:05:15,123 --> 00:05:19,765
Six times pi over eighteen hundred
is pi over three hundred centimetres

107
00:05:19,765 --> 00:05:20,754
per second.

108
00:05:21,354 --> 00:05:22,816
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:22,816 --> 00:05:24,319
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:27,519 --> 00:05:30,356
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:30,756 --> 00:05:31,656
One.

112
00:05:31,231 --> 00:05:36,075
On a turning wheel, which is the same
for every point: omega, or the speed?

113
00:05:36,975 --> 00:05:37,875
Two.

114
00:05:37,527 --> 00:05:41,840
Why must theta be in radians
when you use s equals r theta?

115
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:43,640
Three.

116
00:05:43,332 --> 00:05:47,234
How do you turn sixty turns a
minute into radians per second?

117
00:05:48,334 --> 00:05:52,403
Next time: why does a salad
spinner fling water off the leaves?

118
00:05:52,403 --> 00:05:54,467
We meet centripetal acceleration.
